# V-JEPA-LLVE Project Findings (2026-05-02)

Consolidated technical note covering experiments and analysis through 2026-05-02.
Purpose: durable record decoupled from the long review thread (`VJEPA_LLVE_CODE_REVIEW.md`).

---

## 1. Executive conclusion

V-JEPA 2.1 dense feature conditioning is **load-bearing** for our low-light video
enhancement (LLVE) pipeline, providing roughly **+3-4.5 dB** RGB-PSNR uniformly
across motion levels measured by GT frame difference. Several attempts to add
*supervision* on top of V-JEPA — affinity-consistency loss, RAFT optical-flow
warp loss — produced negative or null results that match V-JEPA 2.1's documented
design intent (encoder for downstream conditioning, not as a target).

The current local performance ceiling on our SDSD-indoor static_np test split is
approximately **23.5 RGB-PSNR (full-frame tiled inference)**. The gap to LLVE-STCD's
reported 28.93 dB is real but cannot be cleanly compared without external
methodology validation (different test sequences, possibly different metric protocol).

**Next-direction recommendation:** consolidate findings → external SOTA comparison
on our pipeline (AR2.A) → only then decide whether to scale data (BVI-RLV) or
improve the pixel decoder.

---

## 2. Current v9.0 architecture and protocol caveats

### 2.1. Architecture

```text
v9.0 model:
  Frozen V-JEPA 2.1 ViT-L encoder (304M frozen)
  Multi-scale dense token cross-attention into pixel decoder (4 scales)
  Pixel branch: BiasFree LN + PixelShuffle + Restormer blocks (361M trainable)
  Local illumination cue injection (5-channel concat at all scales)
  Volume-CFIM bottleneck for cross-frame interaction
  Retinex dual aux head (R̂, L̂) supervised by R̂⊙L̂≈HQ + temporal smoothness

Loss bundle:
  Charbonnier (pix), LPIPS, Retinex compose, L spatial smoothness,
  L temporal smoothness, R adjacent low-res, pred DoD
```

### 2.2. Protocol caveats

The internal training-time validation uses RGB-PSNR at 256×256 center crop, evaluated
as sliding(3) over a 14-sequence SDSD-indoor static_np test split. This **differs**
from LLVE-STCD's published protocol in three ways:

```text
1. PSNR metric:
   ours during training: RGB-256 sliding(3)
   ours under audit:     RGB-PSNR uint8 [0,255], full-frame, tiled inference
   LLVE-STCD:            RGB-PSNR uint8 [0,255], full-frame, direct inference

2. Test sequence list:
   ours:       pair3_2..pair9_2 (14 sequences from static_np release)
   LLVE-STCD:  pair13/15/21/23/31/33/50/52/58/60/68/70 (12 sequences)
   intersection: EMPTY  ← different SDSD release
   
3. Frame protocol:
   ours: first 16 frames per sequence
   LLVE-STCD: N_frames=5 input window, evaluates over more frames per video
```

These differences mean our internal numbers are NOT directly comparable to
LLVE-STCD's published 28.93 even on the same nominal dataset.

### 2.3. Tile-inference variant

Full-frame inference does not fit (1080×1920 model would need ~440 GB VRAM at
our scale). We use 256×256 tiles with 64-pixel cosine-tapered overlap blending.
A direct-vs-tiled sanity check on 512×512 crops shows mean abs difference ≈0.014
and PSNR(direct, tiled) ≈ 34.6 dB — **below** the ≥45 dB acceptance threshold.
All audit numbers in this note are therefore tiled-inference variants, not
faithful proxies for direct full-frame inference.

---

## 3. Positive result: V-JEPA dense conditioning is load-bearing

### 3.1. v9.0-noJEPA ablation

We removed the V-JEPA backbone forward and all cross-attention modules; kept
pixel branch + illumination cues + Retinex heads + losses unchanged. Same data
(SDSD-indoor + SDSD-outdoor mix), same loss balance, same optimizer schedule.

```text
Per RGB-256 sliding(3) on internal val (14 indoor seqs):
  v9.0:        peak 25.07 sliding @ iter 7500
  v9.0-noJEPA: peak 19.22 sliding(1) @ iter 500
              monotonically declining 17.91 → 17.45 → 17.24 thereafter
              stopped at iter 2000 (collapse confirmed)
```

```text
Per RGB-PSNR full-frame tiled audit:
  v9.0:        23.49 mean across 14 indoor seqs
  v9.0-noJEPA: 19.66 mean
  delta:       3.83 dB
```

Without V-JEPA conditioning, pixel branch alone cannot recover scene structure
under our same-recipe training schedule. **V-JEPA cross-attention is structural,
not cosmetic.**

### 3.2. Cost vs benefit

```text
                   v9.0    v9.0-noJEPA    delta
Trainable params:  361.1M  351.9M         -9.2M (cross-attn modules)
Frozen V-JEPA:     304M    0              -304M
GPU peak:          41 GB   29.4 GB        -11.6 GB (V-JEPA backbone fwd)
Step time:         5.13s   4.94s          -4%
Quality (RGB-tile): 23.49   19.66          -3.83 dB
```

12 GB extra VRAM and 4% step time costs us ~3.8 dB on full-frame indoor audit (~4.15 dB on same-schedule RGB-256 sliding). Strong cost-justification to
keep V-JEPA conditioning.

### 3.3. Alignment with V-JEPA 2.1's design

V-JEPA 2.1's "Dense Predictive Loss" specifically optimizes ALL spatial tokens
(visible + masked) to be useful, not just masked tokens. This produces dense,
spatially-localized features at every position — exactly what cross-attention KV
needs. Our v9.0 design pattern matches Meta's downstream-task adaptation pattern
(use the dense features, don't add new objectives on top of them).

---

## 4. Negative result: RAFT/optical-flow warp supervision (v9.1a)

### 4.1. Hypothesis tested

Add a RAFT-small estimated bidirectional optical flow + forward-backward
occlusion mask + warped self-consistency loss on pred and R_hat:
- `pred_warp_weight: 0.05`: ‖pred_t − warp(pred_{t+1}, flow_back)‖ × mask
- `R_warp_weight:    0.10`: ‖R_t − warp(R_{t+1}, flow_back)‖ × mask
- `R_adjacent_weight: 0.01` (lowered from 0.05)

Trained 7500 iters under same v9.0 schedule, all other losses unchanged.

### 4.2. Result

```text
v9.1a peak sliding(3) RGB-256: 23.785 @ iter 7500
v9.0  peak sliding(3) RGB-256: 25.070 @ iter 7500
delta: -1.29 dB (statistically significant given trajectory)
```

Live PSNR diverged early (iter 4000), gap widened over training. Stop/debug rule
triggered (Y-512 < 25.0 by iter 8000).

### 4.3. Diagnostic: P5 RAFT real-flow audit

We ran the warp loss on ground-truth HQ frames using HQ-derived oracle flow.
Even oracle-flow-on-HQ-frames produced **−32%** masked-L1 reduction (i.e. warp
made it WORSE). Bilinear-warp interpolation noise on SDSD-indoor exceeds the
actual frame-to-frame difference, because indoor scenes are mostly static
(GT frame-pair L1 ≈ 0.0056, warp interpolation noise ≈ 0.007).

### 4.4. Conclusion

```text
Image-space RAFT-warp supervision is structurally negative on SDSD-indoor.
This holds for any flow source (LQ-flow, pred-flow, HQ-oracle-flow).
The dataset is too static for warp loss to provide useful supervision.
```

Expected to also be negative on most dataset that has a mix of static + motion
content, because static frames dominate the loss budget. Could be revisited only
for genuinely motion-rich data (BVI-RLV/DID territory).

---

## 5. Negative result: V-JEPA token affinity supervision

### 5.1. Hypothesis tested

We thought V-JEPA tokens carry temporal correspondence information that could
be used as a self-supervision signal: enforce affinity(pred_t, pred_{t+1}) to
be close to affinity(HQ_t, HQ_{t+1}) in token space.

### 5.2. Diagnostic before training

`tools/affinity_diag_v9.py` ran cosine token-affinity at the deepest V-JEPA
layer on 9 clips × 3 frame pairs across LQ / pred(v9.0_best) / HQ:

```text
Mean top-1 cosine:    LQ 0.888  pred 0.869  HQ 0.879
Median displacement:  LQ 1.11   pred 1.15   HQ 0.77 (token units)
closer-to-HQ score (pred vs LQ): −0.0066 (negative — pred slightly farther from HQ)
```

V-JEPA tokens are nearly identical across modalities — the encoder is too
**low-light invariant** to discriminate enhancement quality. A token-affinity
matching loss would have negligible gradient signal at init, and could even
penalize the enhancement direction (since pred is slightly farther from HQ than
LQ is in V-JEPA token space).

### 5.3. Why this is consistent with V-JEPA's design

V-JEPA was trained for masked-feature prediction with the explicit goal of
illumination-invariant, high-level scene representation. The same property that
makes it a good encoder for action recognition makes it a poor signal source
for pixel-level photometric supervision: it doesn't change much when the input
changes only in low-frequency illumination/exposure.

### 5.4. Conclusion

```text
V-JEPA token affinity is mismatched as a supervision target for LLVE.
This is a property of V-JEPA's invariance objective, not a bug in our diagnostic.
Should not be revisited unless using a different encoder family.
```

---

## 6. Motion audit correction (Section 5 of AU4)

### 6.1. Initial result (AQ.A) and its flaw

We bucketed our 14 indoor sequences into static / mild / dynamic by adjacent-frame
mean abs difference of LQ frames (motion_lq) and computed RGB-PSNR per bucket.
Result appeared to show V-JEPA's contribution **decreased** with motion:

```text
Δ(v9.0 − noJEPA) by motion_lq bucket:  6.39 / 4.00 / 1.12 dB
```

This was misinterpreted as evidence against a "V-JEPA helps motion" hypothesis.

### 6.2. The bucketing artifact

Reviewer noted that `motion_lq` is a poor motion proxy on low-light video
because it conflates real motion with low-light noise/flicker. We re-bucketed
using `motion_hq` (GT frame difference). 5 of 14 sequences ended up in different
buckets:

```text
seq         motion_hq  motion_lq  bucket_by_hq  bucket_by_lq
pair34_2    0.004      0.008      static        dynamic     ← OPPOSITE
pair6_2     0.005      0.008      static        dynamic     ← OPPOSITE
pair4       0.005      0.008      mild          dynamic
pair7       0.006      0.005      mild          static
pair3_2     0.009      0.007      dynamic       mild
```

The "dynamic by motion_lq" bucket was contaminated by sequences with high LQ
noise but low GT motion.

### 6.3. Corrected result (AT)

Per-bucket lift over LQ baseline (bucket by motion_hq, RGB-PSNR full-frame tiled):

```text
Bucket    n   lift_v9.0   lift_v9.0b_mix   lift_v9.1a   lift_noJEPA
static    5   14.77       14.93            14.12        11.64
mild      5   14.13       14.45            14.25         9.57
dynamic   4   15.20       14.20            14.88        11.44

Δ(v9.0 − noJEPA) by motion_hq bucket:  3.13 / 4.57 / 3.76 dB
v9.0 lift across buckets:              14.77 → 14.13 → 15.20 (roughly flat)
```

### 6.4. Correlation analysis

Pearson r over 14 sequences:

```text
                  r(lift, motion_hq)   r(lift, motion_lq)
v9.0_best:        +0.165               −0.341
v9.0b_mix_best:   −0.006               −0.428
v9.1a_best:       +0.219               −0.276
v9.0-noJEPA:      +0.143               +0.334  ← OPPOSITE sign on motion_lq
```

All V-JEPA-conditioned models correlate negatively with motion_lq; noJEPA
correlates positively. This sign-flip cleanly confirms motion_lq is measuring
**low-light noise**, not motion: V-JEPA-conditioned models lose lift on noisy
clips (V-JEPA's invariance anchors prediction toward a "clean" estimate that
mismatches noisy LQ); noJEPA gains lift on noisy clips because there's more
LQ→HQ gradient signal to chase.

Caveat (per reviewer AU2/AU3.4): n=14 is small, "GT frame-difference proxy" is
RGB diff not luma. The bucket table itself is the most defensible evidence;
correlation numbers should be read as directional only.

### 6.5. Conclusion

```text
On current SDSD/static_np indoor audit:
  V-JEPA's contribution is approximately motion-neutral when measured by
  GT frame-difference proxy.
  
  No motion-specific behavior is detectable on this split.
  
  motion_lq is a noise/flicker proxy on low-light video and should not be
  used as motion proxy.
```

This does NOT prove V-JEPA can never help motion-rich data on different
benchmarks (BVI-RLV/DID). It says: a temporal V-JEPA adapter is not the next
best engineering branch given current evidence.

---

## 7. Dataset and protocol status

### 7.1. SDSD/static_np ↔ LLVE-STCD test split mismatch

```text
Our static_np test set (14 sequences):
  pair3_2, pair34_2, pair4, pair4_2, pair5, pair5_2,
  pair6, pair6_2, pair7, pair7_2, pair8, pair8_2, pair9, pair9_2

LLVE-STCD published test set (12 sequences):
  pair13, pair15, pair21, pair23, pair31, pair33,
  pair50, pair52, pair58, pair60, pair68, pair70

Intersection: EMPTY
```

Likely from different SDSD release versions or different split files. Until
this is resolved, our local PSNR numbers cannot be directly compared to
LLVE-STCD's published 28.93 dB.

### 7.2. Tiled-inference caveat

All RGB-full-frame audit numbers in this document are tiled-inference
variants, not direct full-frame inference. Per Section 2.3, tile-vs-direct
sanity check shows ~34.6 dB PSNR — meaningful tile-boundary artifacts.

### 7.3. BVI-RLV / DID feasibility

Two motion-rich paired LLVE benchmarks are available:

```text
BVI-RLV (Lin et al. 2024):
  40 scenes (32 train / 8 test)
  31,800 paired frames
  1920×1080 sRGB consumer cam (NOT RAW)
  Two low-light levels (10% and 20% of normal)
  IEEE DataPort open access
  Reported SOTA: BVI-Mamba 31.22 PSNR
  Disk: ~80 GB compressed
  
DID (Fu et al. ICCV 2023):
  Motion-rich dancing scenes
  Strict spatial alignment, multiple exposures
  Available via Baidu Pan
  Less mature codebase, less documented
```

BVI-RLV is the strongly preferred dataset upgrade if/when scaling experiments
become justified.

